% Test cases for the urological supplies policy (urological_supplies.le):
% synthetic claims, one or two per criterion of LCD L33803 and per payment
% condition of dmepos.le and Policy Article A52521, each stating only what
% its record shows. Findings are recorded as the treating practitioner's,
% the reviewer's or the Council's; a finding nobody made leaves the answer
% conditional. Supplies are a one-off or a refill: no rental month. The
% queries: whether the claim is payable (the first query, so "stage" reads
% its checklist), whether it is reasonable and necessary, and what minimal
% change would make it payable.

the target language is: prolog.

the knowledge base urological_supplies cases includes these resources:
    urological_supplies.

the knowledge base urological_supplies cases includes:

query pay is:
    which claim is payable.

query rn is:
    which claim is reasonable and necessary.

query stage is:
    the query fails at which section.

query flip is:
    which minimal change to the scenario makes it the case that
        claim 1 is payable.

% A complete claim for a month of straight intermittent catheters (A4351,
% 150 for a limit of 200) for a permanent urinary retention after a spinal
% cord lesion: the retention documented, the practitioner's judgment of
% permanence, Ann performs the procedure herself, and every documentation
% requirement in the record. The prosthetic benefit asks nothing about the
% residence; no code of the policy is on the CMS face-to-face list.
scenario intermittent_complete is:
    claim 1 is for the catheters.
    the HCPCS code of the catheters is "A4351".
    the catheters is furnished to Ann.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 150.
    the medical record of Ann documents urinary retention.
    the finding permanent impairment of urination about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "neurogenic bladder after a T10 spinal cord lesion in 2019; no recovery of voiding is expected".
    Ann or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    the order for the catheters is dated 2026-02-20.
    the order for the catheters is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the catheters includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the catheters includes order date.
    the order for the catheters includes description of the item.
    the order for the catheters includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the catheters includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the catheters was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-22.
    the catheters was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the catheters.
    pay expects answers ["claim 1 is payable"].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers [].

% Indwelling catheters: one Foley a month for Ben (claim 1, covered); two in
% a month for Cy, whose record documents an obstructed catheter (claim 2,
% covered); three for Dee with nothing in the record and the reviewer
% finding no explanation (claim 3, not covered); an all-silicone catheter
% for Eve's latex sensitivity (claim 4, covered); a specialty catheter for
% Fay where the Council finds its need unsupported (claim 5, not covered);
% a three-way catheter for Gus with no history of obstruction (claim 6, not
% covered).
scenario indwelling_catheters is:
    claim 1 is for the Foley catheter.
    the HCPCS code of the Foley catheter is "A4311".
    the Foley catheter is furnished to Ben.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 1.
    claim 2 is for the second Foley catheter.
    the HCPCS code of the second Foley catheter is "A4311".
    the second Foley catheter is furnished to Cy.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 2.
    the medical record of Cy documents catheter obstruction.
    claim 3 is for the third Foley catheter.
    the HCPCS code of the third Foley catheter is "A4311".
    the third Foley catheter is furnished to Dee.
    claim 3 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 3 is 3.
    the finding excess urological supplies explained about Dee is unsupported, according to the reviewer, because "the record gives no reason for changing the catheter more than once a month".
    claim 4 is for the silicone catheter.
    the HCPCS code of the silicone catheter is "A4344".
    the silicone catheter is furnished to Eve.
    claim 4 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 4 is 1.
    the medical record of Eve documents sensitivity to latex.
    claim 5 is for the specialty catheter.
    the HCPCS code of the specialty catheter is "A4340".
    the specialty catheter is furnished to Fay.
    claim 5 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 5 is 1.
    the finding specialty catheter necessary about Fay is unsupported, according to the Council, because "the physician's notes describe no encrustation, no failed straight catheter and no latex sensitivity; the specialty catheter was the supplier's standard product".
    claim 6 is for the three way catheter.
    the HCPCS code of the three way catheter is "A4346".
    the three way catheter is furnished to Gus.
    claim 6 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 6 is 1.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 2 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 4 is reasonable and necessary"].

% Sterile intermittent catheter kits: Hal lives in a nursing facility
% (claim 1, covered); Ida had two urinary tract infections in the twelve
% months before the kits (claim 2, covered), Jo one (claim 3, not covered);
% Kim's spinal cord injury establishes immunosuppression (claim 4,
% covered); 250 kits for Lee in a nursing facility exceed the 200 the LCD
% allows (claim 5, not covered, no explanation asked).
scenario sterile_kits is:
    claim 1 is for the kits.
    the HCPCS code of the kits is "A4353".
    the kits is furnished to Hal.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 150.
    the residence of Hal is nursing facility.
    Hal or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    claim 2 is for the second kits.
    the HCPCS code of the second kits is "A4297".
    the second kits is furnished to Ida.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 120.
    2 urinary tract infections on intermittent catheterization in the twelve months before the sterile kits are documented for Ida.
    Ida or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    claim 3 is for the third kits.
    the HCPCS code of the third kits is "A4297".
    the third kits is furnished to Jo.
    claim 3 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 3 is 120.
    1 urinary tract infections on intermittent catheterization in the twelve months before the sterile kits are documented for Jo.
    Jo or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    claim 4 is for the fourth kits.
    the HCPCS code of the fourth kits is "A4353".
    the fourth kits is furnished to Kim.
    claim 4 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 4 is 180.
    the medical record of Kim documents spinal cord injury.
    Kim or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    claim 5 is for the fifth kits.
    the HCPCS code of the fifth kits is "A4353".
    the fifth kits is furnished to Lee.
    claim 5 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 5 is 250.
    the residence of Lee is nursing facility.
    Lee or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 2 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 4 is reasonable and necessary"].

% External collection devices for urinary incontinence: 30 male external
% catheters for Mia (claim 1, covered); 60 for Ned, where the Council finds
% the greater utilization unexplained (claim 2, not covered — the
% Council-style unsupported finding); external catheters for Ola, who also
% uses an indwelling catheter (claim 3, not covered); female pouches for
% Pat, 30 for a month (claim 4, covered) and 40 (claim 5, not covered: one
% a day).
scenario external_devices is:
    claim 1 is for the external catheters.
    the HCPCS code of the external catheters is "A4349".
    the external catheters is furnished to Mia.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 30.
    the medical record of Mia documents urinary incontinence.
    claim 2 is for the second external catheters.
    the HCPCS code of the second external catheters is "A4349".
    the second external catheters is furnished to Ned.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 60.
    the medical record of Ned documents urinary incontinence.
    the finding excess urological supplies explained about Ned is unsupported, according to the Council, because "the only support for two catheters a day is the supplier's refill log; the treating physician's notes describe no skin breakdown or leakage".
    claim 3 is for the third external catheters.
    the HCPCS code of the third external catheters is "A4349".
    the third external catheters is furnished to Ola.
    claim 3 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 3 is 30.
    the medical record of Ola documents urinary incontinence.
    the indwelling catheter is furnished to Ola.
    the HCPCS code of the indwelling catheter is "A4311".
    claim 4 is for the female pouches.
    the HCPCS code of the female pouches is "A4328".
    the female pouches is furnished to Pat.
    claim 4 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 4 is 30.
    claim 5 is for the extra female pouches.
    the HCPCS code of the extra female pouches is "A4328".
    the extra female pouches is furnished to Pat.
    claim 5 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 5 is 40.
    the medical record of Pat documents urinary incontinence.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 4 is reasonable and necessary"].

% Supplies used with an indwelling catheter: Ray's vinyl leg bags (claim 1,
% covered) and 5 units of tape (claim 2, covered), 20 units of tape (claim
% 3, over the 10 the LCD allows); a leg bag for Sam, who is bedridden (claim
% 4, not covered), a bedside drainage bag for him (claim 5, covered); Tom's
% vinyl and latex leg bags together (claims 6 and 7, neither covered).
scenario drainage_supplies is:
    claim 1 is for the leg bags.
    the HCPCS code of the leg bags is "A4358".
    the leg bags is an accessory of the Foley catheter.
    the leg bags is furnished to Ray.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 2.
    claim 2 is for the tape.
    the HCPCS code of the tape is "A4450".
    the tape is an accessory of the Foley catheter.
    the tape is furnished to Ray.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 5.
    claim 3 is for the extra tape.
    the HCPCS code of the extra tape is "A4450".
    the extra tape is an accessory of the Foley catheter.
    the extra tape is furnished to Ray.
    claim 3 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 3 is 20.
    the HCPCS code of the Foley catheter is "A4311".
    the Foley catheter is furnished to Ray.
    claim 4 is for the second leg bags.
    the HCPCS code of the second leg bags is "A4358".
    the second leg bags is an accessory of the second Foley catheter.
    the second leg bags is furnished to Sam.
    claim 4 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 4 is 2.
    claim 5 is for the bedside bags.
    the HCPCS code of the bedside bags is "A4357".
    the bedside bags is an accessory of the second Foley catheter.
    the bedside bags is furnished to Sam.
    claim 5 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 5 is 2.
    the HCPCS code of the second Foley catheter is "A4311".
    the second Foley catheter is furnished to Sam.
    Sam is bedridden.
    claim 6 is for the vinyl leg bags.
    the HCPCS code of the vinyl leg bags is "A4358".
    the vinyl leg bags is an accessory of the third Foley catheter.
    the vinyl leg bags is furnished to Tom.
    claim 6 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 6 is 2.
    claim 7 is for the latex leg bag.
    the HCPCS code of the latex leg bag is "A5112".
    the latex leg bag is an accessory of the third Foley catheter.
    the latex leg bag is furnished to Tom.
    claim 7 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 7 is 1.
    the HCPCS code of the third Foley catheter is "A4311".
    the third Foley catheter is furnished to Tom.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 2 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 5 is reasonable and necessary"].

% Reasonable and necessary with its order and delivery, but the record
% documents neither urinary incontinence nor urinary retention — only the
% practitioner's judgment of permanence: the statutory benefit (Policy
% Article A52521) fails at the remedy, and the flip adds either condition.
scenario no_documented_impairment is:
    claim 1 is for the catheters.
    the HCPCS code of the catheters is "A4351".
    the catheters is furnished to Ann.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 150.
    the finding permanent impairment of urination about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "neurogenic bladder after a T10 spinal cord lesion in 2019; no recovery of voiding is expected".
    Ann or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    the order for the catheters is dated 2026-02-20.
    the order for the catheters is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the catheters includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the catheters includes order date.
    the order for the catheters includes description of the item.
    the order for the catheters includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the catheters includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the catheters was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-22.
    the catheters was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the catheters.
    pay expects answers [].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at remedy"].
    flip expects changes [["add: the medical record of Ann documents urinary incontinence"], ["add: the medical record of Ann documents urinary retention"]].

% A retention after hip surgery: the treating practitioner orders catheters
% as if it were permanent, the Council finds it temporary — the statutory
% permanence fails at the remedy.
scenario temporary_retention is:
    claim 1 is for the catheters.
    the HCPCS code of the catheters is "A4351".
    the catheters is furnished to Val.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 120.
    the medical record of Val documents urinary retention.
    the finding permanent impairment of urination about Val is unsupported, according to the Council, because "the retention followed hip arthroplasty and the urologist expected voiding to return within weeks; the condition was not of long and indefinite duration".
    Val or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    the order for the catheters is dated 2026-02-20.
    the order for the catheters is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the catheters includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the catheters includes order date.
    the order for the catheters includes description of the item.
    the order for the catheters includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the catheters includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the catheters was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-22.
    the catheters was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the catheters.
    pay expects answers [].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at remedy"].

% A refill of intermittent catheters, the supply running out on 1 June,
% Ann contacted on 15 May and the catheters delivered on 25 May: within the
% refill windows of dmepos.le. No record of continued need is needed: the
% permanent retention establishes it (Policy Article A52521).
scenario refill is:
    claim 1 is for the catheters.
    the HCPCS code of the catheters is "A4351".
    the catheters is furnished to Ann.
    claim 1 is a refill.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-05-25.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 3 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 450.
    the current supply of the catheters is expected to end on 2026-06-01.
    the beneficiary affirmed the need for the refill of the catheters on 2026-05-15.
    the medical record of Ann documents urinary retention.
    the finding permanent impairment of urination about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "neurogenic bladder after a T10 spinal cord lesion in 2019; no recovery of voiding is expected".
    Ann or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    the order for the catheters is dated 2026-02-20.
    the order for the catheters is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the catheters includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the catheters includes order date.
    the order for the catheters includes description of the item.
    the order for the catheters includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the catheters includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the catheters was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-22.
    the catheters was delivered on 2026-05-25.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the catheters.
    pay expects answers ["claim 1 is payable"].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers [].

% Urethral inserts for female stress incontinence (N39.3, the Article's
% list): Wren, 64, can insert them (claim 1, covered); Xia is on
% anticoagulants (claim 2, not covered).
scenario urethral_inserts is:
    claim 1 is for the inserts.
    the HCPCS code of the inserts is "A4336".
    the inserts is furnished to Wren.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 30.
    Wren is female.
    the age of Wren is 64 years.
    the diagnosis code of Wren is "N39.3".
    Wren or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    claim 2 is for the second inserts.
    the HCPCS code of the second inserts is "A4336".
    the second inserts is furnished to Xia.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 30.
    Xia is female.
    the age of Xia is 70 years.
    the diagnosis code of Xia is "N39.3".
    Xia or a caregiver can perform the procedure.
    the medical record of Xia documents anticoagulant therapy.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
